</figure>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">and <a href="#S3.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ Thinking models excel in chat and creative writing. ‣ 3.2 Results ‣ 3 Thinking Benefits Open-ended Reasoning ‣ Language Models that Think, Chat Better" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>).
The gap over baselines is maximum on chat (WildBench and AlpacaEval2): 3–8 points on average.
They are usually also better at Creative Writing and Factual Question Answering.
To provide a further reference, Table <a href="#S3.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ Thinking models excel in chat and creative writing. ‣ 3.2 Results ‣ 3 Thinking Benefits Open-ended Reasoning ‣ Language Models that Think, Chat Better" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> compares our best model, Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct-RLMT, to four strong models.
Remarkably, despite being <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="10\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>10</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10\times</annotation></semantics></math> smaller than the two open source models, our RLMT model outperforms them both by large margins (9–22 points).
It also <span id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">outperforms GPT-4o</span> on chat and creative writing.
We also compare it to the frontier thinking model Claude-3.7-Sonnet (February 2025, rumored to be 150B+ parameters and post-trained on millions of examples).
Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct-RLMT <span id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">outperforms Claude-3.7-Sonnet (thinking) on AlpacaEval2 and Wildbench</span>, though it is worse on ArenaHardv2, likely due to the high proportion of math and coding.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.T3" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 3: </span>DPO/PPO results for warm-start and zero training. 
<span id="S3.T3.5" class="ltx_inline-block fa fa-lightbulb-o" aria-hidden="true">
</span> shows whether thinking is enabled, with <math id="S3.T3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\checkmark" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>✓</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\checkmark</annotation></semantics></math> denoting RLMT models and <math id="S3.T3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math> denoting RLHF models. The best numbers are <span id="S3.T3.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">bolded</span> and the second are <span id="S3.T3.7" class="ltx_text ltx_underline">underlined</span>. Warm-start + RLMT remains effective with DPO/PPO, but they lag GRPO. The two algorithms are ineffective compared to GRPO for zero training.
</figcaption>
